/a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurbakhsh-singh-of-kalsia"><span class="enc-name">Gurbakhsh Singh (Kalsia)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurbakhsh Singh was a Sikh sardar of the Sandhu Jat clan from the village of Kalsia near Lahore and a member of the Karorsinghia misl.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurbanguly-berdimuhamedow"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gurbanguly-berdimuhamedow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gurbanguly Berdimuhamedow</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurbanguly Berdimuhamedow is a Turkmen politician, a dentist by training, who became President of Turkmenistan after the death of Saparmurat Niyazov in 2006.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurdit-singh-of-ladwa"><span class="enc-name">Gurdit Singh (Ladwa)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurdit Singh was a Sikh sardar who seized Ladwa and nearby lands near Kurukshetra around 1763, when the Sikh misls overran the Mughal province of Sirhind.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/guregen"><span class="enc-name">Güregen</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Güregen, meaning 'royal son-in-law', was a title borne in the Mongol world by men who married into the family of Genghis Khan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurganj"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gurganj.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gurganj</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurganj, also known as Urgench, was a city in Khwarazm in Central Asia and the capital of the Khwarazmian Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurgen-i-of-lori"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gurgen-i-of-lori.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gurgen I of Lori</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurgen, also known as Kiurike I, was the son of King Ashot III of Bagratid Armenia and founder of the Kingdom of Lori, also called Tashir-Dzoraget, in northern Armenia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurgen-king-of-the-iberians"><span class="enc-name">Gurgen, King of the Iberians</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurgen was a Georgian Bagratid prince of Tao-Klarjeti who held the title King of Kings of the Iberians from 994 until his death in 1008.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurgenes"><span class="enc-name">Gurgenes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurgenes was a king of Iberia in eastern Georgia in the 520s, known from the Byzantine historian Procopius.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurindam-dua-belas"><span class="enc-name">Gurindam Dua Belas</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gurindam Dua Belas ('Twelve Gurindam') is a Malay poem of moral and religious advice written by Raja Ali Haji on Penyengat island in 1847.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurjars"><span class="enc-name">Gurjars</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gurjars are a community of north-western India and Pakistan, historically pastoralists and farmers, whose name appears in sources from the sixth century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gurkhas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gurkhas</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gurkhas, or Gorkhas, were originally the soldiers of the Gorkha kingdom, w